-- CISSP Domain 1 expansion part B/4: objective 1.5 (legal/regulatory/compliance - GDPR & privacy law, computer crime law, IP, import/export controls, transborder data flow), 1.6 (investigation types: administrative/criminal/civil/regulatory/industry-standard), 1.7 (policy/standard/procedure/guideline enforcement, maintenance, exceptions) - flashcards ci-f-300..334/ci-a-130..136/ci-y-070..077, questions ci-q-300..339.

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 1.5 core
('ci-f-300','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$What are the core GDPR data subject rights?$$,$$Right of access (obtain a copy of one's data), right to rectification (correct inaccuracies), right to erasure ("right to be forgotten"), right to restrict processing, right to data portability (receive data in a portable format), and right to object (e.g., to direct marketing or profiling).$$),
('ci-f-301','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$What are the six GDPR lawful bases for processing personal data?$$,$$Consent, performance of a contract, compliance with a legal obligation, protection of vital interests, performance of a public task, and legitimate interests of the controller - at least one basis must apply before any processing begins.$$),
('ci-f-302','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$What is "Privacy by Design and by Default" under GDPR?$$,$$Article 25 requires building data protection into systems and processes from the start (by design) and configuring the most privacy-protective settings as the default (by default), rather than bolting privacy on afterward or making users opt in to protection.$$),
('ci-f-303','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$When must an organization appoint a Data Protection Officer (DPO), and how must the DPO operate?$$,$$Required for public authorities, or organizations whose core activities involve large-scale regular/systematic monitoring or large-scale processing of special-category data. The DPO must report to the highest level of management, act independently, and cannot be penalized for performing DPO duties.$$),
('ci-f-304','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$What gives GDPR extraterritorial reach, and what is the maximum fine tier?$$,$$Article 3 applies GDPR to any controller/processor - anywhere in the world - that offers goods/services to, or monitors the behavior of, people in the EU. The highest fine tier is up to EUR20 million or 4% of global annual turnover, whichever is HIGHER.$$),
('ci-f-305','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$How does the CCPA/CPRA consent model differ from GDPR's?$$,$$GDPR generally requires an affirmative lawful basis (often opt-in consent) before processing begins. CCPA/CPRA is primarily an opt-out model - businesses may process and sell/share personal information by default, and consumers must affirmatively exercise their right to opt out.$$),
('ci-f-306','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$What are the three main mechanisms for lawful transborder transfer of personal data out of the EU?$$,$$An adequacy decision (the EU Commission rules a country's laws provide adequate protection), Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs - EU-approved contract terms binding the receiving party to GDPR-equivalent protections), and Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs - a multinational's own DPA-approved internal transfer policy).$$),
('ci-f-307','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$What is the U.S. Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA), and what does it criminalize?$$,$$A 1986 U.S. federal law that criminalizes intentionally accessing a "protected computer" without authorization or in excess of authorization - the primary federal statute used to prosecute hacking and unauthorized-access cases in the U.S.$$),
('ci-f-308','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$What is the difference between EAR and ITAR export controls?$$,$$The Export Administration Regulations (EAR, enforced by Commerce/BIS) govern dual-use items with civilian and potential military use, including certain encryption software. ITAR (enforced by State/DDTC) governs defense articles and services on the U.S. Munitions List - generally stricter, with no civilian-use carve-out.$$),
-- 1.6 core
('ci-f-309','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$What are the five investigation types relevant to CISSP, and who typically conducts each?$$,$$Administrative (internal, by HR/security/legal for policy violations), criminal (law enforcement, for prosecutable crimes), civil (private parties via lawsuit), regulatory (a government agency enforcing its statutes, e.g., SEC or HHS OCR), and industry-standard/private (e.g., a PCI Forensic Investigator engaged under contract, not law).$$),
('ci-f-310','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$What evidentiary standard applies to a criminal investigation, and why is it the highest?$$,$$"Beyond a reasonable doubt" - the prosecution must prove guilt to a near-certainty because a conviction can result in imprisonment, the most severe consequence any of the investigation types can impose.$$),
('ci-f-311','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$What evidentiary standard applies to most civil and regulatory matters?$$,$$"Preponderance of the evidence" - the claim need only be more likely true than not (just over 50%). Some civil claims (e.g., certain fraud allegations) require the higher "clear and convincing evidence" standard, still below the criminal standard.$$),
('ci-f-312','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$What is an administrative investigation, and what is its typical outcome?$$,$$An internal investigation conducted by an organization's own HR, security, or legal staff into a suspected policy or conduct violation. It can result in disciplinary action up to termination, and may be referred to law enforcement or regulators if it uncovers criminal or reportable conduct.$$),
('ci-f-313','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$What is a regulatory investigation, and what powers do regulators typically have?$$,$$An investigation by a government agency enforcing its own statutes and rules (e.g., SEC for securities law, HHS OCR for HIPAA, a state banking regulator, an EU data protection authority for GDPR). Regulators typically hold subpoena or administrative-subpoena power to compel documents and testimony, and can impose fines, consent decrees, or corrective action plans.$$),
('ci-f-314','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$What is an industry-standard/private investigation, and what is a concrete example?$$,$$An investigation conducted under contractual or industry-body rules rather than government law. Example: after a suspected payment card breach, PCI DSS requires engaging a PCI Forensic Investigator (PFI); findings go to the acquiring bank/card brands, and consequences (fines, loss of processing privileges) are contractual, not criminal.$$),
('ci-f-315','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$What is a litigation hold, and when does the duty to issue one arise?$$,$$A legal hold instructs custodians to preserve potentially relevant information and suspend routine deletion. The duty arises as soon as litigation is "reasonably anticipated" - even before a suit is formally filed. Failing to preserve evidence (spoliation) can bring court sanctions, including an adverse-inference instruction to the jury.$$),
('ci-f-316','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$What are the core stages of the e-discovery process (EDRM)?$$,$$Identify (locate potentially relevant data), Preserve (protect it from alteration/deletion), Collect (gather it defensibly), Process (reduce/organize it), Review (assess relevance and privilege), and Produce (turn it over to the opposing party) - roughly the Electronic Discovery Reference Model.$$),
('ci-f-317','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$How does a communicated AUP/monitoring banner affect an employee's "reasonable expectation of privacy"?$$,$$Clearly publishing and having employees acknowledge an Acceptable Use Policy that states company systems are subject to monitoring, with no expectation of privacy, reduces the employee's reasonable expectation of privacy - supporting the admissibility and defensibility of employer-conducted monitoring and investigation of that system.$$),
('ci-f-318','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$Why should legal counsel be looped in early on a sensitive internal investigation?$$,$$Involving counsel early can bring investigation communications and work product under attorney-client privilege and the work-product doctrine, protecting them from discovery if the matter later becomes litigation or a regulatory action - a protection that is far harder to establish retroactively.$$),
('ci-f-319','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$Why must an administrative investigation be handled carefully if it might later become a criminal case?$$,$$Informal, non-forensic evidence handling during an internal (administrative) investigation - e.g., searching a device without imaging it or preserving a chain of custody - can taint or spoil evidence needed for a later criminal prosecution. When escalation is plausible, forensically sound collection (write-blocked imaging, hashing, documented custody) should be used even for what starts as an HR matter.$$),
('ci-f-320','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$Does the Fourth Amendment generally restrict a private employer's search of its own company-owned systems?$$,$$Generally no - the Fourth Amendment restricts unreasonable search and seizure by the GOVERNMENT, not private employers searching their own equipment. It can apply if a private party is acting as an agent of law enforcement (e.g., searching at police direction); otherwise, employment law, published policy, and applicable privacy statutes govern instead.$$),
('ci-f-321','cissp','core',1,'1.6',$$What is a Mutual Legal Assistance Treaty (MLAT), and why does it matter to cybercrime investigations?$$,$$A formal agreement between two countries under which one country's law enforcement can request the other's assistance in gathering evidence or securing cooperation for a criminal investigation. Because cybercrime often spans borders (offshore servers, foreign suspects), an MLAT request is frequently the only lawful path to obtain foreign-held evidence - but it is typically much slower than domestic legal process.$$),
-- 1.7 core
('ci-f-322','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$What are NIST's three policy scope levels: organizational, issue-specific, and system-specific?$$,$$Organizational (program) policy sets the overall, org-wide security intent and authority. Issue-specific policy addresses a particular topic in depth (e.g., an Acceptable Use Policy or remote-access policy). System-specific policy gives technical direction for a particular system or technology platform.$$),
('ci-f-323','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$What is the typical policy lifecycle from creation to retirement?$$,$$Draft -> stakeholder review -> formal approval (by the policy owner/governance body) -> publish and communicate -> enforce -> periodic review -> revise or retire/supersede. Skipping publication or review steps weakens both compliance and legal defensibility.$$),
('ci-f-324','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$What is the difference between a policy owner and a policy custodian?$$,$$The policy owner (typically an accountable executive, e.g., the CISO) has ultimate authority and accountability for the policy's content and outcomes. The policy custodian (often a security or compliance analyst) drafts, maintains, distributes, and tracks the policy day to day on the owner's behalf.$$),
('ci-f-325','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$What is the difference between a standard and a baseline?$$,$$A standard is a mandatory, specific requirement stated broadly (e.g., "all laptops must use AES-256 full-disk encryption"). A baseline is a minimum, tested reference configuration for a specific platform that operationalizes standards into concrete settings (e.g., a CIS Benchmark-hardened Windows image) - a starting point that is then scoped and tailored.$$),
('ci-f-326','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$What elements make a policy exception/waiver request properly governed?$$,$$A documented business or technical justification, review and approval by an appropriate risk owner (not the requester), a compensating control where feasible, and a defined expiration date that forces re-review - an undocumented or open-ended exception is really just unmanaged non-compliance.$$),
('ci-f-327','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$What are the three layers of policy enforcement?$$,$$Technical (automated controls like Group Policy or DLP that enforce compliance proactively), administrative (disciplinary/HR consequences applied after a violation is found), and audit-based (periodic internal/external audits that surface violations the other two layers missed) - layered together for defense in depth.$$),
('ci-f-328','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$Why is a standard enforced through discipline or contract rather than government prosecution?$$,$$A standard is an internal (or industry-body) mandate, not legislation - violating it breaches organizational policy or a contractual obligation (e.g., a PCI DSS requirement), so consequences are administrative (discipline) or contractual (fines, loss of certification), not criminal prosecution, which is reserved for violating actual law.$$),
('ci-f-329','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$What normally triggers an off-cycle policy review, outside the regular annual schedule?$$,$$A major security incident or breach, a new or changed law/regulation, a significant organizational change (merger, new business line), or a newly adopted technology that the current policy doesn't address - any of these can force revision before the next scheduled review.$$),
('ci-f-330','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$Why does requiring signed policy acknowledgment/attestation matter for enforcement?$$,$$A documented, employee-signed acknowledgment that they read and agreed to the policy is key evidence that the requirement was properly communicated - making later disciplinary action (including termination) far more legally defensible than relying on an unread, unacknowledged document.$$),
('ci-f-331','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$What is a compensating control in the context of a policy exception?$$,$$An alternative safeguard that provides equivalent risk reduction when the primary required control genuinely cannot be implemented (e.g., network isolation and enhanced monitoring for a legacy system that cannot support the mandated encryption standard) - it does not remove the requirement, it substitutes for it.$$),
('ci-f-332','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$What is the difference between a zero-tolerance and a risk-based approach to policy enforcement?$$,$$Zero-tolerance permits no exceptions regardless of justification, typically reserved for the highest-risk or compliance-mandated controls (e.g., PCI cardholder data handling). A risk-based approach allows documented, approved exceptions with compensating controls when the residual risk is judged acceptable - most enterprise policies use this model for everything else.$$),
('ci-f-333','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$What information should a governance document's version control carry?$$,$$A version number, effective date, next scheduled review date, and the approving owner/signature, plus a revision history log - this creates an audit trail proving exactly which version of a policy was in force at any given point, which matters heavily during an audit or legal dispute.$$),
('ci-f-334','cissp','core',1,'1.7',$$How do internal security standards typically relate to external frameworks like NIST SP 800-53 or ISO/IEC 27001?$$,$$Organizations commonly build their internal, mandatory standards by adopting and adapting external framework controls - translating a general external control (e.g., an 800-53 access control family or a 27001 Annex A control) into a specific, enforceable internal requirement tailored to their own environment.$$),
-- acronyms
('ci-a-130','cissp','acronym',1,'1.5',$$CCPA$$,$$California Consumer Privacy Act (2018) - a state privacy law giving California residents rights over their personal information, later expanded by the CPRA; enforced on an opt-out (of sale/sharing) model rather than GDPR's opt-in consent approach.$$),
('ci-a-131','cissp','acronym',1,'1.5',$$DPO$$,$$Data Protection Officer - the independent role GDPR requires certain organizations to appoint to oversee data protection compliance; reports to top management and cannot be penalized for performing DPO duties.$$),
('ci-a-132','cissp','acronym',1,'1.5',$$PII$$,$$Personally Identifiable Information - any data that can identify a specific individual (alone or combined with other data); the central category of data that privacy laws like GDPR and CCPA regulate.$$),
('ci-a-133','cissp','acronym',1,'1.5',$$SCC$$,$$Standard Contractual Clauses - EU Commission-approved contract terms that bind a data recipient outside the EU to GDPR-equivalent data protection obligations, one of the lawful mechanisms for transborder data transfer.$$),
('ci-a-134','cissp','acronym',1,'1.6',$$MLAT$$,$$Mutual Legal Assistance Treaty - a formal agreement letting one country's law enforcement request evidence or cooperation from another country's authorities during a cross-border criminal investigation.$$),
('ci-a-135','cissp','acronym',1,'1.7',$$AUP$$,$$Acceptable Use Policy - an issue-specific policy defining permitted and prohibited use of an organization's systems and networks; the document typically referenced when an employee is disciplined for misuse.$$),
('ci-a-136','cissp','acronym',1,'1.7',$$SOP$$,$$Standard Operating Procedure - a documented, step-by-step set of instructions for performing a routine task consistently; the "procedure" layer of the policy/standard/procedure/guideline hierarchy, updated far more frequently than the policy above it.$$),
-- feynman
('ci-y-070','cissp','feynman',1,'1.5',$$Explain, in plain terms, why GDPR can apply to a U.S. company that has no office in Europe.$$,$$Model answer: GDPR isn't about where a company is based - it's about whose data is being processed and why. Article 3 gives GDPR extraterritorial reach: if a U.S. company offers goods or services to people in the EU, or tracks/monitors their behavior (say, an e-commerce site that ships to Germany or a website using EU visitor analytics), it's processing EU residents' personal data and falls under GDPR - even without a single EU employee or server. The trigger is the targeting/monitoring of EU residents, not the company's location. That's why "we're not based in Europe" is not a defense against GDPR compliance obligations.$$),
('ci-y-071','cissp','feynman',1,'1.6',$$Explain the difference in evidentiary standard between criminal, civil, and regulatory investigations using a courtroom analogy.$$,$$Model answer: Think of it as three different bars an accuser has to clear. Criminal is the highest bar - "beyond a reasonable doubt" - because the state can take away someone's liberty, so society demands near-certainty before conviction. Civil is a much lower bar - "preponderance of the evidence" - just tip the scale past 50%, because the worst outcome is usually a money judgment, not prison. Regulatory sits with civil in most systems - agencies also generally use preponderance (or a similarly modest "substantial evidence" standard) because their remedies are fines and corrective orders, not incarceration. Same facts can clear the civil/regulatory bar and still fail to clear the much higher criminal bar - which is why a company can lose a civil suit or regulatory action over conduct that a prosecutor could never convict on.$$),
('ci-y-072','cissp','feynman',1,'1.6',$$Explain why an HR-led administrative investigation should still use forensically sound evidence handling.$$,$$Model answer: An administrative investigation often starts small - just HR reviewing whether an employee violated policy - but you don't always know at the start where it will end up. If the underlying conduct turns out to be criminal (theft of trade secrets, fraud) or triggers a lawsuit, the very same evidence HR collected may need to hold up in court. If HR just poked around a laptop without imaging the drive, hashing files, or logging who touched what and when, a defense attorney can argue the evidence was altered or unreliable, and it may get thrown out. Treating collection as if it might someday go to court - write-blocked imaging, documented chain of custody - costs little extra up front and preserves the option to escalate later without losing the evidence.$$),
('ci-y-073','cissp','feynman',1,'1.6',$$Explain why a company can face serious consequences from a PCI industry investigation with no criminal charges ever filed.$$,$$Model answer: PCI DSS isn't a law - it's a contractual requirement the card brands (Visa, Mastercard, etc.) impose on any merchant or processor that wants to accept their cards. When a card-data breach is suspected, the contract (not a court) requires engaging a PCI Forensic Investigator, and the findings go to the acquiring bank and card brands, not a prosecutor. The consequences that follow - fines, mandatory remediation, increased transaction fees, or even losing the ability to process cards - come from breach of that private contract, exactly like a landlord evicting a tenant for breaking a lease. No government ever has to prove a crime for a business to be hurt badly by this process; the accountability mechanism is entirely private and contractual.$$),
('ci-y-074','cissp','feynman',1,'1.6',$$Explain why a signed AUP with a "no expectation of privacy" clause helps an employer defend a workplace investigation.$$,$$Model answer: Courts and juries look at whether a person had a reasonable expectation of privacy before deciding if monitoring or searching their activity was fair. If an employee never agreed to anything and reasonably assumed their work email was private, an employer digging through it looks intrusive and can face pushback (or even liability) for the search. But if the employee signed an Acceptable Use Policy that clearly says "company systems are monitored, you have no expectation of privacy in your use of them," that expectation is gone by their own informed agreement - the employer can point to a specific document the employee acknowledged. It doesn't make monitoring unlimited, but it substantially strengthens the legal footing for using what's found in an investigation or disciplinary action.$$),
('ci-y-075','cissp','feynman',1,'1.7',$$Explain why a security policy needs a documented exception process instead of just being quietly ignored when inconvenient.$$,$$Model answer: If people can simply ignore a policy whenever it's inconvenient, the policy stops meaning anything - it becomes a suggestion, not a mandate, and nobody can tell real risk-based decisions apart from plain non-compliance. A documented exception process forces the inconvenience into the open: someone has to justify why the standard can't be met, someone with actual risk authority has to approve it knowingly, and (ideally) a compensating control offsets the gap. Crucially, it also has an expiration date, so a "temporary" workaround can't quietly become permanent. That paper trail is exactly what an auditor or regulator wants to see - not zero deviations, but every deviation consciously owned by someone accountable.$$),
('ci-y-076','cissp','feynman',1,'1.7',$$Explain the difference between a standard and a baseline using a real configuration example.$$,$$Model answer: A standard states the mandatory rule in general terms - "all Windows servers must be hardened against a recognized industry benchmark." That's a requirement, but it doesn't tell an admin exactly which registry keys or services to disable. A baseline is the concrete, tested answer to that question for a specific platform - e.g., the CIS Benchmark hardened image for Windows Server 2022, with every setting specified. You could say the standard sets the destination and the baseline is the actual map showing how to get there for one particular system type; different systems (a database server vs. a web server) might use different baselines to satisfy the very same overarching standard.$$),
('ci-y-077','cissp','feynman',1,'1.7',$$Explain why consistent enforcement matters when defending a termination for a policy violation.$$,$$Model answer: Imagine two employees violate the same acceptable-use rule, but only one gets fired - the other gets a warning. If the fired employee sues, the company's strongest argument ("we enforce this policy strictly") collapses the moment the other case comes up, because it wasn't actually enforced strictly - it was enforced selectively. That inconsistency invites the employee to argue the real reason for termination was something else entirely (discrimination, retaliation), using the uneven enforcement as evidence. A policy that is written down, communicated, acknowledged, AND applied the same way to everyone who breaks it is what actually holds up under legal challenge; any one of those four pieces missing weakens the whole defense.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 1.5 legal, regulatory, compliance
('ci-q-300','cissp',1,'1.5','mcq',1,$q$An EU resident asks a company to permanently delete all personal data the company holds about them. Which GDPR right are they exercising?$q$,$q$["Right to data portability","Right to erasure","Right to rectification","Right to restrict processing"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The right to erasure (the "right to be forgotten") lets a data subject request deletion of their personal data, subject to certain exceptions (e.g., legal retention obligations). Portability covers receiving data in a transferable format; rectification covers correcting inaccurate data; restriction limits processing without deleting the data.$q$),
('ci-q-301','cissp',1,'1.5','mcq',1,$q$A company processes customer data to fulfill purchase orders without ever asking for consent. Under GDPR, which lawful basis most likely justifies this processing?$q$,$q$["Consent","Performance of a contract","Vital interests","Public task"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Processing necessary to fulfill a contract with the data subject (e.g., shipping what they ordered) is justified under the "performance of a contract" lawful basis - consent is not the only, or even the most natural, basis for routine order fulfillment. Vital interests covers life-or-death situations; public task applies to official/government functions.$q$),
('ci-q-302','cissp',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$A new mobile app collects location data continuously by default, requiring users to manually turn tracking off in settings if they don't want it. Which GDPR principle does this design violate?$q$,$q$["Privacy by Design and by Default","Data minimization only, not privacy by default","The right to erasure","Data sovereignty"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Privacy by Design and by Default (Article 25) requires the most privacy-protective settings to be the DEFAULT - users should have to opt in to broader collection, not opt out of it. Collecting by default and making users disable tracking inverts that requirement.$q$),
('ci-q-303','cissp',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$A hospital system's core activity involves large-scale, ongoing processing of patient health records. Under GDPR, what is this organization generally required to do?$q$,$q$["Appoint a Data Protection Officer (DPO)","Obtain a software export license","File for patent protection on its health records","Register as a public task authority"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Large-scale processing of special-category data (health records) as a core activity triggers the GDPR requirement to appoint an independent DPO who reports to top management. Export licenses, patents, and "public task" registration are unrelated requirements.$q$),
('ci-q-304','cissp',1,'1.5','mcq',3,$q$A U.S.-only company with no EU offices or servers runs targeted ads based on the browsing behavior of visitors it knows are located in France. Does GDPR apply to this company?$q$,$q$["No, GDPR never applies outside the EU","Yes, because it is monitoring the behavior of people in the EU","No, GDPR only applies to companies with EU revenue","Yes, but only if the company has more than 500 EU customers"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Article 3's extraterritorial scope applies GDPR to any organization - anywhere - that monitors the behavior of people located in the EU (such as behavioral ad targeting), regardless of the company's location, revenue, or customer count thresholds.$q$),
('ci-q-305','cissp',1,'1.5','mcq',3,$q$A multinational retailer is fined for a GDPR violation. Its global annual turnover is $2 billion. Under the highest GDPR fine tier, what is the fine calculated against?$q$,$q$["A flat EUR20 million only, capped regardless of revenue","The higher of EUR20 million or 4% of global annual turnover","1% of EU-only revenue","A fixed EUR1 million per violation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The top GDPR fine tier is up to EUR20 million or 4% of GLOBAL annual turnover, whichever amount is HIGHER - for a company with billions in global revenue, 4% of turnover would substantially exceed the EUR20 million flat figure.$q$),
('ci-q-306','cissp',1,'1.5','mcq',1,$q$Which best describes how CCPA's consumer privacy model differs from GDPR's general approach?$q$,$q$["CCPA is opt-out (of sale/sharing) by default; GDPR generally requires an affirmative lawful basis before processing","CCPA and GDPR use identical opt-in consent models","CCPA applies only to EU residents","GDPR has no consent requirement at all"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$CCPA/CPRA lets businesses process and sell/share data by default, with consumers exercising a right to opt OUT. GDPR requires an affirmative lawful basis (frequently opt-in consent) to exist before processing can lawfully begin at all.$q$),
('ci-q-307','cissp',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$A U.S. cloud provider wants to legally transfer EU customer data to its U.S. data centers without relying on an adequacy decision. Which mechanism can it use?$q$,$q$["Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs)","Filing a patent application","Obtaining an ITAR export license","Registering a trademark"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$In the absence of an adequacy decision covering the destination, Standard Contractual Clauses (or Binding Corporate Rules for intra-group transfers) are the standard lawful mechanism to bind the receiving party to GDPR-equivalent protections. Patents, ITAR licenses, and trademarks are unrelated to data transfer.$q$),
('ci-q-308','cissp',1,'1.5','multi',3,$q$Which TWO statements about export controls on security technology are correct? (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["EAR governs dual-use items, including certain encryption software, administered by the Department of Commerce","ITAR governs commercial dual-use items with no military application","The Wassenaar Arrangement is a binding UN treaty enforced by international courts","ITAR governs defense articles and services on the U.S. Munitions List, administered by the Department of State"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$EAR (Commerce/BIS) covers dual-use goods including some encryption; ITAR (State/DDTC) covers defense articles/services on the Munitions List - the opposite of "no military application." The Wassenaar Arrangement is a voluntary, multilateral export-control regime among participating states, not a binding UN treaty with court enforcement.$q$),
('ci-q-309','cissp',1,'1.5','ordering',2,$q$Order these potential consequences of a privacy/security violation from LEAST severe to MOST severe.$q$,$q$["An unenforced guideline deviation with no consequence","A PCI DSS contractual fine from the card brands","A GDPR regulatory fine from a data protection authority","A CFAA criminal prosecution resulting in imprisonment"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Guidelines are recommended, not enforced, so deviation alone typically carries no consequence. A PCI DSS violation triggers a private contractual fine. A GDPR violation triggers a government regulatory fine (up to 4% of global turnover). A CFAA conviction is a criminal matter that can result in imprisonment - the most severe outcome of the four.$q$),
('ci-q-310','cissp',1,'1.5','matching',2,$q$Match each legal/regulatory instrument to what it PRIMARILY governs.$q$,$q${"left":["GDPR","CFAA","ITAR","PCI DSS"],"right":["EU personal-data privacy and processing","Unauthorized access to U.S. protected computers","Export of defense articles and services","Private industry security requirements for payment card data"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$GDPR is EU personal-data law; the CFAA is the U.S. federal computer-crime statute for unauthorized access; ITAR controls export of defense-related articles/services; PCI DSS is a contractual industry standard for payment card security - not government law at all.$q$),
-- 1.6 investigation types
('ci-q-311','cissp',1,'1.6','mcq',1,$q$An employee is suspected of violating the company's remote-work security policy, and the company's own HR and security teams investigate without involving law enforcement. What type of investigation is this?$q$,$q$["Criminal investigation","Administrative investigation","Regulatory investigation","Civil investigation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An investigation conducted internally by an organization's own HR/security/legal staff into a policy or conduct violation is an administrative investigation - it can lead to internal discipline, independent of any law enforcement or court involvement.$q$),
('ci-q-312','cissp',1,'1.6','mcq',1,$q$Local police obtain a warrant and seize a suspect's computers as part of an investigation that could result in the suspect's imprisonment. What type of investigation is this?$q$,$q$["Administrative investigation","Criminal investigation","Industry-standard investigation","Civil investigation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Investigations conducted by law enforcement under criminal procedure (warrants, potential imprisonment as an outcome) are criminal investigations, held to the "beyond a reasonable doubt" evidentiary standard.$q$),
('ci-q-313','cissp',1,'1.6','mcq',1,$q$A company sues a former employee in court for breach of a non-disclosure agreement, seeking monetary damages. What evidentiary standard will the court apply?$q$,$q$["Beyond a reasonable doubt","Preponderance of the evidence","Absolute certainty","No evidentiary standard applies"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Civil lawsuits (like a breach-of-contract/NDA claim) are decided on a preponderance of the evidence - the claim need only be more likely true than not. "Beyond a reasonable doubt" is reserved for criminal cases.$q$),
('ci-q-314','cissp',1,'1.6','mcq',2,$q$A securities regulator opens a formal inquiry into whether a public company failed to properly disclose a data breach to investors. What type of investigation is this?$q$,$q$["Civil investigation","Regulatory investigation","Industry-standard investigation","Administrative investigation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A securities regulator is a government agency enforcing securities law within its statutory authority - this is a regulatory investigation, which can compel documents/testimony via subpoena and result in fines or consent decrees.$q$),
('ci-q-315','cissp',1,'1.6','mcq',2,$q$After a suspected payment card data breach, a merchant's acquiring bank requires it to engage a PCI Forensic Investigator (PFI) under its card-acceptance contract. What type of investigation is this?$q$,$q$["Criminal investigation","Regulatory investigation","Industry-standard/private investigation","Civil investigation"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$PCI DSS is a contractual industry standard, not government law - engaging a PFI and reporting findings to the card brands is an industry-standard/private investigation, with consequences that are contractual (fines, loss of processing privileges) rather than legal prosecution.$q$),
('ci-q-316','cissp',1,'1.6','matching',2,$q$Match each investigation type to its typical evidentiary standard.$q$,$q${"left":["Criminal investigation","Civil investigation","Regulatory investigation","Administrative investigation"],"right":["Beyond a reasonable doubt","Preponderance of the evidence (civil claims)","Preponderance of the evidence / substantial evidence (agency standard)","An internal, organization-defined standard of proof (no fixed legal threshold)"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Criminal investigations require proof beyond a reasonable doubt - the highest bar, since a conviction may result in imprisonment. Civil investigations and most regulatory investigations use the lower preponderance (or an agency's substantial-evidence) standard. Administrative investigations aren't bound by a fixed legal evidentiary standard at all - the organization defines what convinces it discipline is warranted, subject to employment-law fairness requirements.$q$),
('ci-q-317','cissp',1,'1.6','mcq',1,$q$A company reasonably anticipates being sued over a data breach, even though no lawsuit has been filed yet. What obligation is immediately triggered?$q$,$q$["A duty to issue a litigation hold and preserve potentially relevant evidence","A duty to notify a regulator within 72 hours in all cases","A duty to immediately terminate the employees involved","No obligation until a complaint is formally filed in court"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The duty to preserve evidence (litigation hold) arises as soon as litigation is reasonably anticipated, not only once a suit is formally filed. Failing to preserve relevant evidence at that point risks spoliation sanctions later.$q$),
('ci-q-318','cissp',1,'1.6','mcq',3,$q$A company fails to issue a litigation hold after reasonably anticipating a lawsuit, and relevant emails are auto-deleted by a routine retention policy. What is this failure called, and what can a court do about it?$q$,$q$["Spoliation of evidence; the court can impose sanctions such as an adverse-inference instruction","Data minimization; the court will praise the company's privacy practices","Legitimate interest processing; no consequence follows","Data portability failure; the company must pay a GDPR fine"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Losing evidence a party had a duty to preserve is spoliation. Courts can sanction the responsible party, including instructing the jury to assume the destroyed evidence was unfavorable to them (an adverse-inference instruction).$q$),
('ci-q-319','cissp',1,'1.6','mcq',1,$q$An employer publishes and requires employees to sign an Acceptable Use Policy stating company systems are monitored and there is no expectation of privacy in their use. What does this primarily help establish?$q$,$q$["A reduced reasonable expectation of privacy, supporting later monitoring/investigation","A guarantee that no investigation can ever be legally challenged","An automatic waiver of all employee legal rights","A requirement that law enforcement approve all searches"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A clearly communicated and acknowledged AUP reduces the employee's reasonable expectation of privacy in their use of company systems, which strengthens (though does not absolutely guarantee) the legal defensibility of subsequent monitoring or investigation of that system.$q$),
('ci-q-320','cissp',1,'1.6','mcq',3,$q$Why might a company's general counsel want to be looped in at the very start of a sensitive internal investigation, rather than after it concludes?$q$,$q$["To ensure the investigation communications and work product can be protected under attorney-client privilege from the outset","Because only lawyers are legally allowed to interview employees","Because counsel involvement is required by the Fourth Amendment","Because it eliminates the need for any documentation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Bringing counsel in early can extend attorney-client privilege and work-product protection over the investigation's communications and materials - a protection that is difficult or impossible to establish retroactively once the investigation is already underway.$q$),
('ci-q-321','cissp',1,'1.6','mcq',2,$q$A private employer, acting entirely on its own and not at the direction of police, searches an employee's company-issued laptop as part of a workplace investigation. Does the Fourth Amendment restrict this search?$q$,$q$["Yes, it applies equally to private employers and government agents","Generally no - the Fourth Amendment restricts government search and seizure, not private employers acting on their own","Yes, but only for employees who have worked more than one year","No, because private employers are never subject to any privacy law"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The Fourth Amendment restricts unreasonable search and seizure by the GOVERNMENT. A private employer searching its own equipment, acting independently rather than as a law enforcement agent, is generally not restricted by it - though other laws (employment law, state privacy statutes, published policy) may still apply.$q$),
('ci-q-322','cissp',1,'1.6','mcq',3,$q$U.S. investigators need evidence stored on a server located in another country for a cross-border cybercrime case. Which formal mechanism allows them to lawfully request that country's cooperation?$q$,$q$["A Mutual Legal Assistance Treaty (MLAT) request","A Standard Contractual Clause (SCC)","A PCI Forensic Investigator engagement","A Binding Corporate Rule (BCR)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An MLAT is the formal treaty mechanism allowing one country's law enforcement to request evidence-gathering assistance or cooperation from another country for a criminal investigation. SCCs and BCRs govern lawful transborder data transfer for privacy compliance, not criminal evidence requests; a PFI is a private/industry investigator, not a cross-border legal mechanism.$q$),
('ci-q-323','cissp',1,'1.6','multi',2,$q$Which TWO of the following are true characteristics of a civil investigation? (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["It is decided on a preponderance-of-the-evidence standard","It can result in the losing party being sent to prison","It typically proceeds between private parties through the discovery process","It always requires a government prosecutor to bring the case"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Civil matters use the preponderance standard and proceed between private parties via discovery (subpoenas, depositions, e-discovery). They cannot result in imprisonment (a criminal-only outcome) and are not brought by a government prosecutor - a private plaintiff, not the state, initiates a civil case.$q$),
('ci-q-324','cissp',1,'1.6','multi',3,$q$Which TWO of the following distinguish an administrative investigation from a criminal investigation? (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["It is conducted by the organization's own staff rather than law enforcement","It generally uses a lower evidentiary threshold than beyond a reasonable doubt","It can result in imprisonment of the subject","It is governed by the same constitutional search-and-seizure protections as a police investigation in every respect"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Administrative investigations are run internally (HR/security/legal, not police) and use a lower standard than "beyond a reasonable doubt" (the criminal threshold). They cannot themselves result in imprisonment, and private employer searches are not bound by the same constitutional protections that restrict government criminal investigations.$q$),
('ci-q-325','cissp',1,'1.6','ordering',2,$q$Order the e-discovery (EDRM) stages as data moves from a live system to being handed over in litigation.$q$,$q$["Identify potentially relevant data","Preserve it from alteration or deletion","Collect it in a forensically defensible manner","Process and organize the collected data","Review it for relevance and privilege","Produce it to the opposing party"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5]$q$::jsonb,$q$The Electronic Discovery Reference Model flows Identify -> Preserve -> Collect -> Process -> Review -> Produce. Skipping or reordering these stages (e.g., collecting before preserving) risks spoliation or an incomplete, indefensible production.$q$),
-- 1.7 policy, standards, procedures, guidelines
('ci-q-326','cissp',1,'1.7','mcq',1,$q$A company publishes a document stating "All employees must use the corporate VPN when accessing internal systems remotely," addressing this one specific topic in depth. Which NIST policy scope level is this?$q$,$q$["Organizational (program) policy","Issue-specific policy","System-specific policy","Baseline"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A document focused on one particular topic (remote access) is an issue-specific policy - narrower than an organization-wide program policy, and not the technical, platform-level detail of a system-specific policy or baseline.$q$),
('ci-q-327','cissp',1,'1.7','mcq',2,$q$The CISO is ultimately accountable for the content and outcomes of the information security policy, even though a compliance analyst drafts and maintains the document day to day. What is the CISO's role called?$q$,$q$["Policy custodian","Policy owner","Policy auditor","Policy subject"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The policy owner holds ultimate authority and accountability for the policy, typically an accountable executive like the CISO. The custodian (the analyst here) handles day-to-day drafting and maintenance on the owner's behalf.$q$),
('ci-q-328','cissp',1,'1.7','mcq',1,$q$An organization adopts the CIS Benchmark for Windows Server as its required, tested starting configuration for all new servers of that type. What is this reference configuration called?$q$,$q$["A guideline","A baseline","A procedure","A regulation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A baseline is a minimum, tested reference configuration for a specific platform that operationalizes a broader standard into concrete settings - exactly what a CIS Benchmark hardened image provides.$q$),
('ci-q-329','cissp',1,'1.7','mcq',3,$q$A business unit cannot meet a mandated encryption standard on a legacy system for technical reasons. What should happen BEFORE the system is allowed to continue operating out of compliance?$q$,$q$["Nothing - undocumented deviations are acceptable if the system is old","A documented exception request with justification, appropriate risk-owner approval, and ideally a compensating control","Immediate decommissioning of the system with no review","Automatic exemption from all future audits"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A properly governed exception requires documented justification, approval by an appropriate risk owner (not the requester), and ideally a compensating control plus an expiration date - an undocumented deviation is simply unmanaged non-compliance, not a legitimate exception.$q$),
('ci-q-330','cissp',1,'1.7','mcq',1,$q$A Group Policy Object automatically enforces a minimum password length before an account can be created. What layer of policy enforcement does this represent?$q$,$q$["Administrative enforcement","Technical enforcement","Audit-based enforcement","Legal enforcement"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Technical enforcement uses automated controls (like a GPO) to enforce compliance proactively and prevent non-compliant configurations from being created in the first place - distinct from administrative (after-the-fact discipline) or audit-based (periodic review) enforcement.$q$),
('ci-q-331','cissp',1,'1.7','mcq',2,$q$Why is violating an internal security standard typically handled through discipline or contract remedies rather than criminal prosecution?$q$,$q$["Because internal standards are legislation enforced by government prosecutors","Because a standard is an organizational or contractual mandate, not law, so violating it is not a crime","Because standards can never be enforced at all","Because only guidelines can be enforced through discipline"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A standard is set by the organization (or an industry body via contract, like PCI DSS) - it is not government legislation, so breaching it is a policy or contract violation handled through internal discipline or contractual remedies, not criminal prosecution reserved for violating actual law.$q$),
('ci-q-332','cissp',1,'1.7','mcq',3,$q$Which event would most likely force an off-cycle review of a security policy, ahead of its next scheduled annual review?$q$,$q$["A routine, uneventful year with no changes","A major security breach exposing a gap the current policy doesn't address","An employee reading the policy for the first time","A scheduled software license renewal"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A major incident that reveals a gap in policy coverage is a classic off-cycle review trigger, alongside new/changed regulations, major organizational change, or newly adopted technology the policy doesn't yet address.$q$),
('ci-q-333','cissp',1,'1.7','mcq',1,$q$An organization wants any future disciplinary action for policy violations to be as legally defensible as possible. Which practice best supports that goal?$q$,$q$["Keeping the policy verbal and informal so it can flex case by case","Requiring employees to read and sign an acknowledgment of the written policy","Publishing the policy but never checking whether anyone read it","Enforcing the policy only against employees management dislikes"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A documented, signed acknowledgment is strong evidence the requirement was properly communicated and understood, making later discipline far more defensible. A verbal/informal policy, unchecked publication, or selective enforcement all weaken - rather than support - legal defensibility.$q$),
('ci-q-334','cissp',1,'1.7','mcq',2,$q$A legacy application cannot be patched to meet a mandated vulnerability-remediation standard. The security team instead isolates it on a restricted network segment with enhanced monitoring. What is this isolation and monitoring an example of?$q$,$q$["A compensating control","A policy waiver with no conditions","A new baseline replacing the standard","An audit finding"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A compensating control is an alternative safeguard providing equivalent risk reduction when the primary required control can't be implemented - it doesn't remove the underlying requirement, it substitutes protection for it while the gap exists.$q$),
('ci-q-335','cissp',1,'1.7','multi',3,$q$Which TWO of the following help explain why an organization applies zero-tolerance enforcement to cardholder-data handling but a risk-based model to most other internal standards? (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Cardholder-data requirements are externally mandated by a contract (PCI DSS) that permits no deviation","Internally-set standards allow the organization to judge and accept residual risk case by case","Zero tolerance is legally required for every possible security control","Risk-based exceptions are prohibited by federal law in all cases"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$PCI DSS is an external, contractual mandate with no acceptable deviation for cardholder-data controls, which is why organizations apply zero tolerance there, while most internally-set standards allow a risk-based approach because the organization itself can judge and formally accept residual risk. Neither "zero tolerance for everything" nor "risk-based exceptions being illegal" is true.$q$),
('ci-q-336','cissp',1,'1.7','mcq',2,$q$An auditor reviewing a security policy document wants to confirm exactly which version was in effect at the time of a prior incident six months ago. What element of the document makes this verifiable?$q$,$q$["The document's color scheme","Version control metadata - version number, effective date, and revision history","The number of pages in the document","The font used in the document"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Version control metadata (version number, effective date, next review date, approving owner, and a revision history log) creates the audit trail needed to prove exactly which version of a governance document was in force at a given point in time.$q$),
('ci-q-337','cissp',1,'1.7','matching',2,$q$Match each governance-document characteristic to the document type it MOST describes.$q$,$q${"left":["High-level, rarely-changed statement of mandatory intent approved by senior management","Updated frequently as tools and day-to-day processes evolve","Recommended practice whose deviation triggers no disciplinary action","Mandatory, specific, and often maps to an external framework like NIST 800-53"],"right":["Policy","Procedure","Guideline","Standard"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$A policy is the high-level, mandatory, infrequently-revised statement of intent. Procedures are the step-by-step instructions updated most often as tools/processes change. Guidelines are recommended, non-mandatory practices whose deviation carries no formal consequence. Standards are mandatory and specific, and are often built by adapting an external framework's controls into a concrete internal requirement.$q$),
('ci-q-338','cissp',1,'1.7','multi',1,$q$Which TWO of the following are required elements of a properly governed policy exception? (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["A documented justification reviewed by an appropriate risk owner","A defined expiration date that forces re-review","Silent approval by the requester themselves with no other sign-off","Permanent, indefinite validity once granted"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A properly governed exception needs a documented justification approved by someone with actual risk authority (not the requester self-approving) and a defined expiration that forces re-review - an indefinite, self-approved exception is unmanaged non-compliance, not governance.$q$),
('ci-q-339','cissp',1,'1.7','ordering',2,$q$Order the stages of a governance document's lifecycle, from creation through ongoing maintenance.$q$,$q$["Draft the document","Route it for stakeholder review","Obtain formal approval from the policy owner","Publish and communicate it to the organization","Enforce it through technical, administrative, and audit-based controls","Periodically review and revise or retire it"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5]$q$::jsonb,$q$The policy lifecycle runs draft -> review -> approval -> publish/communicate -> enforce -> periodic review/revise-or-retire. Skipping steps (e.g., enforcing before publishing, or never revisiting the document) undermines both compliance and legal defensibility.$q$);
